# Mandatory Audit Before Completion

Never claim a task, feature, or build is "done" without first completing a full self-audit. Every implementation must be verified before marking complete.

## Required Audit Steps

1. **Read every file you created or modified** — no exceptions. Verify the logic does what you think it does.
2. **Check for dead code** — if you built a hook, component, or utility, confirm it is actually imported and called somewhere. Unused code = unfinished work.
3. **Trace the data flow end-to-end** — follow props from parent to child, server action to client handler, DB column to UI render. Confirm nothing is disconnected.
4. **Verify z-index and layering** — any overlay, modal, or confetti must render above everything it needs to. Check competing z-indices across the codebase.
5. **Check animation performance** — if an animated component renders in a list, confirm it won't fire N simultaneous animations on page load. Animations should only run when meaningful (user-triggered, viewport entry), not on every server-rendered item.
6. **Verify refs and event targets** — if a ref is assigned to multiple elements, only the last assignment wins. Confirm the ref points to the element the user actually interacts with.
7. **Run `tsc --noEmit`** — the build must compile with zero type errors.
8. **Run lints on all edited files** — fix any linter errors you introduced.

## What "Done" Means

A feature is done when:
- Every new component is imported and rendered somewhere real
- Every new hook is called in at least one consumer
- Every new DB column is read and written by actual code paths
- Every new server action is invoked from a client component
- The TypeScript compiler and linter both pass clean
- You have personally re-read the final state of every changed file

If any of these are false, the feature is not done — keep working.
